WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:05.460
[music]

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.420
Hi, I'm Tina Hesman Saey.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.280
I'm the molecular biology reporter

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.540
for Science News magazine.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.440
CRISPR is a hot new molecular biology tool,

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.840
that you may have heard of,

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.480
because everybody is using it to edit genomes,

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.960
to fix genes, and break genes,

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.700
and do all kinds of things.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.860
CRISPR started out as a bacterial defense system

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.560
that sniffs out viruses

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.820
and chops them up into little pieces.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.780
But some very clever scientists have re-engineered

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.000
the molecule so you can search out specific genes

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.580
and change them.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:46.360
According to most scientists CRISPR can do anything,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.100
but that's not exactly true, but it can do a lot of stuff.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.620
They've already used it

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.560
to create more muscular beagles,

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.700
to repaint the wings on butterflies,

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.640
to create a mushroom

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.680
that doesn't brown when you cut it

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.540
and even to re-engineer some immune cells

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:12.040
so they will go and kill tumor cells in cancer.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.500
So CRISPR is currently mostly a research tool,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.700
but it's starting to get into helping people.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.000
In 2017, you can expect, for instance,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.140
to see clinical trials for cancer starting out with CRISPR.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:38.120
And also, possibly, bringing CRISPR back to its roots

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:43.180
to combat viruses like HIV and hepatitis.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.000
There's a question about whether

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.520
using CRISPR is ethical in a lot of different situations.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.840
On the one hand you have the problem of

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.460
engineering designer babies perhaps,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.020
by changing their genes with CRISPR.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.460
And a lot of people are really uncomfortable with that.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:05.040
But even if you just limit it to curing diseases,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.840
for instance, some people may not be able

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.040
to afford a CRISPR therapy.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:13.580
So you have to decide who should have access,

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.010
how much should it cost.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.450
A lot of my beat is concerned with...

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.920
on the one hand we can do all these incredible things

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.160
with tools like CRISPR,

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.280
but on the other hand should we really do them?

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.240
[music]

